{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"institution":[{"name":"bioRxiv"}],"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,18]],"date-time":"2026-09-18T04:52:49Z","timestamp":1789707169655,"version":"4.0.1"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2018,4,5]]},"group-title":"Genomics","reference-count":35,"publisher":"openRxiv","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":[],"accepted":{"date-parts":[[2018,4,5]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                <jats:p>\n                  Loss-of-function mutations in\n                  <jats:italic>BRCA1<\/jats:italic>\n                  confer a predisposition to breast and ovarian cancer. Genetic testing for mutations in the\n                  <jats:italic>BRCA1<\/jats:italic>\n                  gene frequently reveals a missense variant for which the impact on the molecular function of the BRCA1 protein is unknown. Functional BRCA1 is required for homology directed repair (HDR) of double-strand DNA breaks, a key activity for maintaining genome integrity and tumor suppression. Here we describe a multiplex HDR reporter assay to simultaneously measure the effect of hundreds of variants of BRCA1 on its role in DNA repair. Using this assay, we measured the effects of ~1,700 amino acid substitutions in the first 302 residues of BRCA1. Benchmarking these results against variants with known effects, we demonstrate accurate discrimination of loss-of-function versus benign variants. We anticipate that this assay can be used to functionally characterize BRCA1 missense variants at scale, even before the variants are observed in results from genetic testing.\n                <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1101\/295279","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2018,4,5]],"date-time":"2018-04-05T18:04:06Z","timestamp":1522951446000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":4,"title":["A multiplexed homology-directed DNA repair assay reveals the impact of ~1,700 BRCA1 variants on protein function"],"prefix":"10.64898","author":[{"given":"Lea M.","family":"Starita","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Muhtadi M.","family":"Islam","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tapahsama","family":"Banerjee","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Aleksandra I.","family":"Adamovich","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Justin","family":"Gullingsrud","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Stanley","family":"Fields","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jay","family":"Shendure","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jeffrey D.","family":"Parvin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"54368","reference":[{"key":"2019071821415349000_295279v1.1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1097\/GIM.0b013e318226fc15"},{"key":"2019071821415349000_295279v1.2","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"3067","DOI":"10.1245\/s10434-017-5959-3","article-title":"Clinical Decision-Making in Patients with Variant of Uncertain Significance in BRCA1 or BRCA2 Genes","volume":"24","year":"2017","journal-title":"Ann. Surg. Oncol."},{"key":"2019071821415349000_295279v1.3","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1093\/nar\/gkt1113"},{"key":"2019071821415349000_295279v1.4","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1172\/JCI70196"},{"key":"2019071821415349000_295279v1.5","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"2539","DOI":"10.2147\/OTT.S102569","article-title":"Distinct implications of different BRCA mutations: Efficacy of cytotoxic chemotherapy, PARP inhibition